<p class="lt-chem-335305">Determine the stoichiometry of the metal-ligand complex and its formation constant.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Instrumental_Analysis_(LibreTexts)/25:_Voltammetry/25.07:_Voltammetry_with_Ultramicroelectrodes" class="mt-section"><span id="Solution_4"></span><h6 class="lt-chem-335305 editable">Solution</h6>

<p class="lt-chem-335305">We begin by calculating values of \(\Delta E_{1/2}\) using Equation \ref{app13}, obtaining the values in the following table.</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th class="mt-align-center" scope="col">[<em>L</em>] (M)</th>
            <th class="mt-align-center" scope="col">\(\Delta E_{1/2}\)<sub><em> </em></sub>(V vs. SCE)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="[L] (M)">0.020</td>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="\(\Delta E_{1/2}\) (V vs. SCE)">&ndash;0.268</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="[L] (M)">0.040</td>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="\(\Delta E_{1/2}\) (V vs. SCE)">&ndash;0.286</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="[L] (M)">0.060</td>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="\(\Delta E_{1/2}\) (V vs. SCE)">&ndash;0.297</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="[L] (M)">0.080</td>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="\(\Delta E_{1/2}\) (V vs. SCE)">&ndash;0.304</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="[L] (M)">0.100</td>
            <td class="mt-align-center lt-chem-335305" data-th="\(\Delta E_{1/2}\) (V vs. SCE)">&ndash;0.310</td>
        </tr>
    </thead>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-335305">Figure \(\PageIndex{5}\) shows the resulting plot of \(\Delta E_{1/2}\) as a function of log[<em>L</em>]. A linear regression analysis gives the equation for the straight line as</p>

<p class="lt-chem-335305">\[\triangle E_{1 / 2}=-0.370 \mathrm{V}-0.0601 \log {[L]} \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-335305">From Equation \ref{app14} we know that the slope is equal to &ndash;0.05916<em>p</em>/<em>n</em>. Using the slope and <em>n </em>= 2, we solve for <em>p </em>obtaining a value of 2.03 &asymp; 2. The complex&rsquo;s stoichiometry, therefore, is <em>ML</em><sub>2</sub>. We also know, from Equation \ref{app14}, that the <em>y</em>-intercept is equivalent to &ndash;(0.05916/<em>n</em>)log\(\beta_p\). Solving for \(\beta_2\) gives a formation constant of \(3.2 \times 10^{12}\).</p>
</div></div></section>

<figure><img alt="Determination of the stoichiometry and formation constant for a metal-ligand complex using the data in Example 25.7.4." class="internal default" style="width: 357px; height: 295px;" width="357px" height="295px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/189936/Figure11.55.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=357&amp;height=295" />
<figcaption><a name="figure11.4.22">Figure \(\PageIndex{5}\)</a>. Determination of the stoichiometry and formation constant for a metal-ligand complex using the data in Example \(\PageIndex{4}\).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p class="lt-chem-335305">Cyclic voltammetry is one of the most powerful electrochemical techniques for exploring the mechanism of coupled electrochemical and chemical reactions. The treatment of this aspect of cyclic voltammetry is beyond the level of this text, although you can consult this chapter&rsquo;s additional resources for additional information.</p>
</div></div>

